{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc5oe2TytbJdcm29FgF4", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here's a regex for an ISO 8601 calendar date in the `YYYY-MM-DD` format:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\n- `\\d{4}`: a four-digit year\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])`: a month from 01 to 12\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])`: a day from 01 to 31\n- `^\u2026$`: the whole string must be the date. Remove these anchors to find dates inside longer text, and use `\\b` in their place if you need word boundaries.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01`. It rejects `2026-13-01`, `2026-10-32` and `26-10-01`.\n\n**Limitation:** the regex checks the format only, not whether the date exists. It would accept `2026-02-30` and `2026-04-31`. To catch those, also parse the string after it matches. For example, in Python:\n\n```python\nimport re\nfrom datetime import date\n\nISO_DATE = re.compile(r\"^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\")\n\ndef is_iso_date(s: str) -> bool:\n    if not ISO_DATE.match(s):\n        return False\n    try:\n        date.fromisoformat(s)\n        return True\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n```\n\nIf you only need the shape and don't care about valid month or day ranges, `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$` is enough."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 348, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24380, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 348}, "output_tokens": 516, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d9a00888-42b9-4dae-8637-c23f75a5b049", "uuid": "2869b475-af2a-4fe2-8a73-a5e841652cdf", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T19:39:17.076Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfc5odpoyrz9Z1U4dytWL"}
